<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AquariusOS — put "The Pour" in GNOME's wallpaper picker
  =============================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR, IN PLAIN ENGLISH

  Two completely separate things have to be true for a wallpaper to work
  properly, and it is easy to do one and forget the other:

    1. IT IS THE WALLPAPER ON A NEW MACHINE.
       That is settled elsewhere, in
       /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/zz1-aquarius-10-look.gschema.override.

    2. IT IS IN THE LIST when somebody opens Settings > Appearance.
       That is this file, and nothing else does it.

  Without this file the wallpaper still appears on a fresh install — but the
  moment somebody browses the picker to look at the other options, ours is not
  among them, and there is no way back to it except knowing the file path. That
  is a small thing that feels broken.

  =============================================================================
  THE PIECES

  <filename>       the LIGHT picture. GNOME shows this while the desktop is in
                   its light appearance, which on AquariusOS is the default —
                   the Ice colourway.
  <filename-dark>  the DARK picture, shown when somebody switches to dark. This
                   pairing is what makes one entry in the picker follow the
                   light/dark switch instead of being two separate wallpapers.
  <options>zoom    fill the screen and crop what does not fit, rather than
                   stretching the picture out of shape.
  <pcolor>         the flat colour painted behind the picture for the fraction
                   of a second before it loads. Matched to the wallpaper's own
                   ground so that moment is invisible.

  ⚠️ THE TWO PATHS BELOW MUST MATCH the ones in zz1-aquarius-10-look.gschema.override.
  They are the same two files named in two places, and a rename that only
  updates one of them gives a desktop that comes up a flat colour with no error.
  build_files/gnome-desktop.sh compares them and fails the build if they have
  drifted apart, so this is checked rather than remembered.

  The pictures themselves are rendered from SVG sources in branding/wallpapers/
  by branding/render-wallpaper.sh. Never hand-edit the PNGs.
  =============================================================================
-->
<wallpapers>
  <wallpaper deleted="false">
    <name>The Pour</name>
    <filename>/usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-ice-3840x2160.png</filename>
    <filename-dark>/usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-midnight-3840x2160.png</filename-dark>
    <options>zoom</options>
    <shade_type>solid</shade_type>
    <pcolor>#EAF1F8</pcolor>
    <scolor>#DFEAF4</scolor>
  </wallpaper>
</wallpapers>
